'''Allen Banks & Staward Gorge'''  is a 194-acre [[National Trust for Places of Historic Interest or Natural Beauty|National Trust]] property in the [[England|English]] county of [[Northumberland]].

It is a [[Victorian era|Victorian]] garden in a gorge of the [[River Allen, Northumberland|River Allen]] cutting through woodland. The ruins of [[Staward Peel]], a medieval [[peel tower]], stand on a promontory above the gorge. The property has been designated a [[Site of Special Scientific Interest]] for its rich flora and fauna. There is a large suspension bridge which has been ruined by the flooding of January 2005.

